.TH REVM 1 "October 2026" "revm" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
revm \- run Linux commands in a libkrun microVM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B revm run [--id NAME]
[options] -- command [args...]
.br
.B revm podman --volume NAME [--id NAME]
[options]
.br
.B revm exec --id NAME
[--tty=auto|always|never] -- command [args...]
.br
.B revm
ls | inspect | stop | wait | logs | rm | volume | cache | completion
.SH DESCRIPTION
revm runs the supplied argv in a fresh Alpine system environment. Input and
output stream without waiting for EOF. Without a PTY, stdout and stderr remain
separate. Each session name permits one running instance.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --version
Print version, source commit and build date.
.TP
.B --cpus N, --memory MB
CPU and memory allocation. Defaults: up to four CPUs, one quarter of available
memory bounded to 512-2048 MiB. Minimum memory: 512 MiB.
.TP
.B --tty=auto|always|never
Allocate a PTY. Auto requires foreground terminal stdin and terminal stdout.
Input EOF does not inject Ctrl-D.
.TP
.B --stop-timeout DURATION
Grace period for cancellation, default 10s. A second signal accelerates cleanup.
.TP
.B --id NAME
Retain named session records and logs. Without it, successful runs remove records;
failures and interruptions retain them until revm rm.
.TP
.B --mount HOST:GUEST[,ro]
Share a host directory. Writes persist on the host.
.TP
.B --raw-disk PATH[,mnt=GUEST][,version=LABEL]
Attach an external persistent raw/ext4 disk. Existing disks are never replaced
because a version is missing or mismatched.
.TP
.B --container-disk PATH[,version=LABEL]
Use an external Podman storage disk; mutually exclusive with --volume NAME.
.SH SESSION MANAGEMENT
.B ls --json
lists sessions; 
.B inspect NAME
prints configuration and last result as JSON.
.B wait NAME
returns the selected instance's recorded exit status;
.B wait --ready --timeout 30s NAME
waits for guest readiness.
.B stop NAME
requests shutdown and waits for resource release.
.B logs [--guest] [-f] NAME
reads instance logs.
.B rm NAME
removes a stopped session, preserving volumes.
.SH DATA
Runtime sockets use XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, with a short private /tmp fallback.
Private rootfs copies use XDG_CACHE_HOME/revm/instances.
Logs and results use XDG_STATE_HOME/revm/sessions.
Volumes use XDG_DATA_HOME/revm/volumes.
Unset cache, state and data roots default to ~/.cache, ~/.local/state and
~/.local/share. Roots may not overlap.
.PP
.B volume create NAME
creates independent persistent storage, selected by podman --volume NAME.
.B volume ls --json
lists managed volumes.
.B volume rm NAME
explicitly deletes an unused volume.
.B cache prune
removes inactive resources with verified ownership, preserving legacy data.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 means success; 2 means invalid invocation; 1 means an operational failure.
Guest exit codes are preserved, including 126 and 127. Signals return 128 plus
the Linux signal number. A host cancellation retains its first signal. Unknown
results never count as success. Diagnostics go to stderr; structured command
output goes to stdout.
.SH EXAMPLES
printf 'hello\en' | revm run -- cat
.br
revm run --mount "$PWD:/work" --workdir /work -- make test
.br
revm completion zsh
.SH SEE ALSO
Use revm COMMAND --help for all flags and share/doc/revm for detailed contracts.
